f"},"seconds":35.54688000679016,"seed":568294847568155975,"stop":"eos","text":"**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).**\nWe use the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\) to rewrite the equation in terms of \\(\\cos x\\):\n\\[2(1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0.\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0,\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by -1, we have:\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(\\cos x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\cos x\\):\n\\[2y^2 - 3y + 1 = 0.\\]\nThe quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\) gives:\n\\[y = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\nSo, the solutions are:\n\\[y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\nThus, \\(\\cos x = 1\\) or \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n- For \\(\\cos x = 1\\), the solution in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) is \\(x = 0\\).\n- For \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) and \\(x = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nCombining all the solutions from both cases, we have \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). The sum of these solutions is:\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi.\\]\nThus, the sum of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5\\pi}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5\\pi}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\), we start by using the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nNext, we can factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nWe can rearrange this to:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0.\\]\nThis equation gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\),\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\).**\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).**\nWe use the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\) to rewrite the equation in terms of \\(\\cos x\\):\n\\[2(1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0.\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0,\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by -1, we have:\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(\\cos x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\cos x\\):\n\\[2y^2 - 3y + 1 = 0.\\]\nThe quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\) gives:\n\\[y = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\nSo, the solutions are:\n\\[y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\nThus, \\(\\cos x = 1\\) or \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n- For \\(\\cos x = 1\\), the solution in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) is \\(x = 0\\).\n- For \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) and \\(x = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nCombining all the solutions from both cases, we have \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). The sum of these solutions is:\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi.\\]\nThus, the sum of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5\\pi}\\).","id":"precalculus:64"}
